From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Builder <builder@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hdmitx: register the boot parameter handlers as module
 parameters

Same defect as 0004, a different file. hdmitx_boot_parameters.c registers
seven command line handlers with __setup(): hdmitx=, frac_rate_policy=,
hdr_priority=, hdmichecksum=, config_csc_en=, edid_check= and dsc_policy=.
It stubs the macro for a modular build at the top of the file:

    #ifdef MODULE
    #undef __setup
    #define __setup(_str, _fn)
    #endif

CONFIG_AMLOGIC_MEDIA_MODULE=m here, so MODULE is defined, every __setup()
line collapses to nothing, and all seven parse functions become static
functions with no reference left. The tree adds -Werror unconditionally for
CONFIG_AMLOGIC_DRIVER, so the file does not compile at all:

    hdmitx_boot_parameters.c:133:12: error: 'parse_hdmitx_boot_para'
        defined but not used [-Werror=unused-function]

and six more like it.

Upstream already wrote the fix -- ODROID_HDMITX_DEFINE_STORED_PARAM,
ODROID_HDMITX_DEFINE_VALUE_PARAM and ODROID_HDMITX_REGISTER_PARAM, which
register all seven as module_param_cb() and so give every handler a caller
again -- but put it behind #ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON. That
symbol is a promptless bool at arch/arm64/Kconfig.platforms:399 selected
only by ARCH_MESON_ODROIDC5, which this board config does not set, so the
whole block is compiled out and the build breaks.

So this patch does what 0004 does: it removes the guard and nothing else.
The code that results is the code upstream already ships for its own
boards. Removing the guard cannot regress a configuration that did set
CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON, because there the block was already being
compiled.

This is the last of the four files in common_drivers that stub __setup()
and hide their replacement behind that symbol. The other three are
vout_serve.c, vout2_serve.c and vout3_serve.c, all handled by 0004.

Reachability, and one thing left alone deliberately:

With the guard removed the parameters are reachable as aml_media.hdmitx=,
aml_media.frac_rate_policy= and so on, because KBUILD_MODNAME for this
file is aml_media -- the same module the vout parameters in 0004 land in.
boot/boot.ini currently passes the bare, unprefixed spelling
"hdmitx=,${colorattribute}", which is the built-in form and does not reach
a module parameter; the vout entries on the same line are already written
with the aml_media. prefix. That line is not changed here: boot.ini is
outside the scope of this change, and whether the colour attribute is
actually needed on this board has not been tested. It is recorded so the
mismatch is not mistaken for an oversight.

Runtime effect is NOT VERIFIED. This patch is what makes the file compile.
No board has run this kernel, so whether registering these parameters
changes anything about HDMI output is unknown and must not be assumed.
---
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/hdmi_tx_connector/hdmitx_boot_parameters.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/hdmi_tx_connector/hdmitx_boot_parameters.c
@@ -304,7 +304,12 @@
 }
 __setup("dsc_policy=", hdmitx_boot_dsc_policy);
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON
+/*
+ * Module parameter support, un-gated for the same reason as vout_serve.c:
+ * this file is linked into aml_media, which is a module here, so every
+ * __setup() above expands to nothing and the seven parse handlers are
+ * left as static functions with no caller, which -Werror rejects.
+ */
 static char odroid_hdmitx_param[64];
 
 static int odroid_hdmitx_get_string(char *buf, const struct kernel_param *kp)
@@ -415,5 +420,3 @@
 			     "ODROID HDMI EDID check policy for aml_media");
 ODROID_HDMITX_REGISTER_PARAM(dsc_policy, &tx_params.dsc_policy,
 			     "ODROID HDMI DSC policy for aml_media");
-
-#endif
